### Footnotes

1. If you want another explainer, Twilio's “[What Is IVR (Interactive Voice Response) for Call Centers?](https://www.twilio.com/en-us/blog/ivr-what-it-is-and-how-it-benefits-contact-center)” covers IVR systems from the perspective of UJET: menus that listen for keypad presses or speech, then route you to the right department or human agent. It even mentions a choose-your-own-adventure road trip that Amber Case and Ben Greenstein built, where you steer with your keypad.
1. The side project is on GitHub at [ayukumar261/ringback](https://github.com/ayukumar261/ringback). It runs LiveKit and a custom Go worker on a DigitalOcean Droplet, bridges audio from Twilio's Elastic SIP Trunking to an ElevenLabs agent, and then streams the transcript to a dashboard. The ElevenLabs agent uses DTMF signaling through a `send_dtmf` tool, which is where the `PublishDataPacket` code later in this post lives.
1. Some of those Muse calls weren't made by an AI at all. Jason Koebler at 404 Media reported, in "[Meta Tests Muse AI Agent Calls That Are Actually Made By Humans in a Call Center](https://www.404media.co/meta-tests-muse-ai-agent-calls-that-are-actually-made-by-humans-in-a-call-center/)," that Muse can hand a request off to a trained human in a call center, who places the call for you, and some testers didn't find out until afterward.
1. Tracks and WebRTC data channels don't even use the same protocol. Audio and video tracks travel over SRTP (Secure Real-time Transport Protocol), the encrypted version of RTP. While, WebRTC data channels travel over SCTP (Stream Control Transmission Protocol).
1. The internet never promised to deliver packets in the order you sent them. Packets can take different routes, sit in different queues, or get resent after being lost, so your second message can beat your first message. To guarantee order, the receiver has to hold on to anything that shows up early until the missing message arrives, which is why in-order delivery adds delay.
1. Partially reliable means the sender only tries so hard. You either cap how many times a lost message gets retransmitted (`maxRetransmits`) or how long the sender keeps trying (`maxPacketLifeTime`, in milliseconds), but you can't set both. LiveKit's Go SDK sets `MaxRetransmits` to 1 on its `lossy` WebRTC data channel, so a lost message gets one retry.
